# Records - Field Decisions

**Purpose:** Decisions about the field API in `@art-js/records` — naming, resolution, rename, form selection, the string-write route, and the read model.

## Decision: Canonical Field Names

**Status:** Adopted

**Decision:** Fields are addressed by field name — one address space, no label fallback, no dual mode. A canonical field name matches `/^[a-z][a-zA-Z0-9]*$/`, and only names supplied through the API must be canonical; API-created fields carry the derived label of their name. Extracted names come from the stored label as-is: never rejected for failing the grammar, never rewritten by a value update. A stored label does not round-trip by design; the only required round trip is name → derived label → name.

## Decision: The name → field Index

**Status:** Adopted

**Decision:** Extraction builds one name → field index per record in document order; the first occurrence of a name wins and later duplicates stay in the section, shadowed. Name-addressed access resolves through the index; no access path scans the live section for a label — the repair helpers operate on caller-owned copies, never on live state — and no access path converts a label at access time. Removing the indexed field, by delete or by a rename that vacates the name, promotes the next duplicate, which keeps its own stored label. A label that resolves to a reserved name aborts construction, so the document cannot be extracted until the label is fixed. Access guards follow the name classes: reserved names reject every operation, the canonical grammar gates creation only, and a delete that removes nothing reports failure.

## Decision: Rename by Name

**Status:** Adopted

**Decision:** Renaming operates on names, never labels, and validates before mutating anything: both names are checked for reservation, the source must exist, and the target must be canonical and unoccupied — in that order, with the collision check last so a rejected rename leaves the record untouched. On success the field is relabelled to the derived label of the target name, its position is preserved, and the old spelling is forgotten. The source name is not held to the canonical grammar — it may be any extracted name — while the target must be.

## Decision: Automatic Field Form

**Status:** Adopted

**Decision:** Whether a field is inline or block is chosen from the value being set — the caller never selects it, and no overload, option, or method takes a form. Content that is empty or purely phrasing, including a single parsed paragraph, becomes inline; any block-level content becomes block. Writing to an existing field replaces its value and converts its form in place when the classification changes, preserving name, tags, and position. `Document`, field, and section containers are rejected as values.

## Decision: Strings Parse as Markdown

**Status:** Adopted

**Decision:** Every string input is parsed as markdown rather than stored as a literal text node, so markdown forms survive set → save → read. Numbers and booleans are coerced to string and take the same route, preserving scalar writes; any other non-string value is rejected. The API provides separate methods for the string route and for the value route, and the proxy accepts both. Text containing markdown characters must be escaped or passed as a value. One string mechanism, so the same string behaves identically through either route.

## Decision: Reads Split Into Markdown, Text, and Raw Value

**Status:** Adopted

**Decision:** The API provides three separate reads of a field value: markdown, as the default access; text, preserving the previous markup-stripped rendering; and the raw value as a deep clone of the field's value — a clone, never the live object, so a consumer cannot desynchronise the record by mutating what it read. Consumers that displayed the default read see markdown markers after the change and move to the text read for the previous behaviour. The expanded accessors are reserved names, so the set of document labels that abort extraction grows accordingly.

## Decision: Copy on read

**Status:** Adopted

**Decision:** `record.__section` is a per-access snapshot the caller owns — every access returns a deep clone of the live section, current at the moment of access, and mutations to it never reach the record — while the capsule keeps mutating the live section internally for its own field operations.

## Decision: Repair by replacing entire record.

**Status:** Adopted

**Context:** Multitple fields with the same label, or colliding field names derived from labels, are not accessible via the record (first field name wins). An API for repairing obscured fields must be provided.

**Decision:** Repair runs on `record.__section` working copy: the two helpers (`getObscuredFields`, `renameObscuredField`) are pure over the copy, and deleting a duplicate is a direct `children` edit on the copy, not needing the record. Every apply crosses the collection: `replaceRecordSection` validates the incoming copy, applies it by content-copy — never an object swap, so document membership and anchor identity survive — and re-extracts the record with a fresh capsule (compatible with future reactive `capsuleFactory`).
